verse	12	4	38	@@And Elisha came again to Gilgal: and @9there was@7 a dearth in the land; and the sons of the prophets @9were@7 sitting before him: and he said unto his servant, Set on the great pot, and seethe pottage for the sons of the prophets.
verse	12	4	39	@@And one went out into the field to gather herbs, and found a wild vine, and gathered thereof wild gourds his lap full, and came and shred @9them@7 into the pot of pottage: for they knew @9them@7 not.
verse	12	4	40	@@So they poured out for the men to eat. And it came to pass, as they were eating of the pottage, that they cried out, and said, O @9thou@7 man of God, @9there is@7 death in the pot. And they could not eat @9thereof. @7
verse	12	4	41	@@But he said, Then bring meal. And he cast @9it@7 into the pot; and he said, Pour out for the people, that they may eat. And there was no harm in the pot.
verse	12	4	42	And there came a man from Baalshalisha, and brought the man of God bread of the firstfruits, twenty loaves of barley, and full ears of corn in the husk thereof. And he said, Give unto the people, that they may eat.
verse	12	4	43	@@And his servitor said, What, should I set this before an hundred men? He said again, Give the people, that they may eat: for thus saith the LORD, They shall eat, and shall leave @9thereof. @7
verse	12	4	44	@@So he set @9it@7 before them, and they did eat, and left @9thereof,@7 according to the word of the LORD.
verse	12	5	1	@@Now Naaman, captain of the host of the king of Syria, was a great man with his master, and honourable, because by him the LORD had given deliverance unto Syria: he was also a mighty man in valour, @9but he was@7 a leper.
verse	12	5	2	And the Syrians had gone out by companies, and had brought away captive out of the land of Israel a little maid; and she waited on Naaman's wife.
verse	12	5	3	@@And she said unto her mistress, Would God my lord @9were@7 with the prophet that @9is@7 in Samaria! for he would recover him of his leprosy.
verse	12	5	4	@@And @9one@7 went in, and told his lord, saying, Thus and thus said the maid that @9is@7 of the land of Israel.
verse	12	5	5	@@And the king of Syria said, Go to, go, and I will send a letter unto the king of Israel. And he departed, and took with him ten talents of silver, and six thousand @9pieces@7 of gold, and ten changes of raiment.
verse	12	5	6	@@And he brought the letter to the king of Israel, saying, Now when this letter is come unto thee, behold, I have @9therewith@7 sent Naaman my servant to thee, that thou mayest recover him of his leprosy.
verse	12	5	7	@@And it came to pass, when the king of Israel had read the letter, that he rent his clothes, and said, @9Am@7 I God, to kill and to make alive, that this man doth send unto me to recover a man of his leprosy? wherefore consider, I pray you, and see how he seeketh a quarrel against me.
verse	12	5	8	@@And it was @9so,@7 when Elisha the man of God had heard that the king of Israel had rent his clothes, that he sent to the king, saying, Wherefore hast thou rent thy clothes? let him come now to me, and he shall know that there is a prophet in Israel.
verse	12	5	9	So Naaman came with his horses and with his chariot, and stood at the door of the house of Elisha.
verse	12	5	10	And Elisha sent a messenger unto him, saying, Go and wash in Jordan seven times, and thy flesh shall come again to thee, and thou shalt be clean.
verse	12	5	11	But Naaman was wroth, and went away, and said, Behold, I thought, He will surely come out to me, and stand, and call on the name of the LORD his God, and strike his hand over the place, and recover the leper.
verse	12	5	12	@@@9Are@7 not Abana and Pharpar, rivers of Damascus, better than all the waters of Israel? may I not wash in them, and be clean? So he turned and went away in a rage.
verse	12	5	13	@@And his servants came near, and spake unto him, and said, My father, @9if@7 the prophet had bid thee @9do some@7 great thing, wouldest thou not have done @9it?@7 how much rather then, when he saith to thee, Wash, and be clean?
verse	12	5	14	Then went he down, and dipped himself seven times in Jordan, according to the saying of the man of God: and his flesh came again like unto the flesh of a little child, and he was clean.
verse	12	5	15	@@And he returned to the man of God, he and all his company, and came, and stood before him: and he said, Behold, now I know that @9there is@7 no God in earth, but in Israel: now therefore, I pray thee, take a blessing of thy servant.
verse	12	5	16	@@But he said, @9As@7 the LORD liveth, before whom I stand, I will receive none. And he urged him to take @9it;@7 but he refused.
verse	12	5	17	And Naaman said, Shall there not then, I pray thee, be given to thy servant two mules' burden of earth? for thy servant will henceforth offer neither burnt offering nor sacrifice unto other gods, but unto the LORD.
verse	12	5	18	@@In this thing the LORD pardon thy servant, @9that@7 when my master goeth into the house of Rimmon to worship there, and he leaneth on my hand, and I bow myself in the house of Rimmon: when I bow down myself in the house of Rimmon, the LORD pardon thy servant in this thing.
verse	12	5	19	And he said unto him, Go in peace. So he departed from him a little way.
verse	12	5	20	@@But Gehazi, the servant of Elisha the man of God, said, Behold, my master hath spared Naaman this Syrian, in not receiving at his hands that which he brought: but, @9as@7 the LORD liveth, I will run after him, and take somewhat of him.
verse	12	5	21	@@So Gehazi followed after Naaman. And when Naaman saw @9him@7 running after him, he lighted down from the chariot to meet him, and said, @9Is@7 all well?
verse	12	5	22	@@And he said, All @9is@7 well. My master hath sent me, saying, Behold, even now there be come to me from mount Ephraim two young men of the sons of the prophets: give them, I pray thee, a talent of silver, and two changes of garments.
verse	12	5	23	@@And Naaman said, Be content, take two talents. And he urged him, and bound two talents of silver in two bags, with two changes of garments, and laid @9them@7 upon two of his servants; and they bare @9them@7 before him.
verse	12	5	24	@@And when he came to the tower, he took @9them@7 from their hand, and bestowed @9them@7 in the house: and he let the men go, and they departed.
verse	12	5	25	@@But he went in, and stood before his master. And Elisha said unto him, Whence @9comest thou,@7 Gehazi? And he said, Thy servant went no whither.
verse	12	5	26	@@And he said unto him, Went not mine heart @9with thee,@7 when the man turned again from his chariot to meet thee? @9Is it@7 a time to receive money, and to receive garments, and oliveyards, and vineyards, and sheep, and oxen, and menservants, and maidservants?
verse	12	5	27	@@The leprosy therefore of Naaman shall cleave unto thee, and unto thy seed for ever. And he went out from his presence a leper @9as white@7 as snow.
verse	12	6	1	And the sons of the prophets said unto Elisha, Behold now, the place where we dwell with thee is too strait for us.
verse	12	6	2	Let us go, we pray thee, unto Jordan, and take thence every man a beam, and let us make us a place there, where we may dwell. And he answered, Go ye.
verse	12	6	3	And one said, Be content, I pray thee, and go with thy servants. And he answered, I will go.
verse	12	6	4	So he went with them. And when they came to Jordan, they cut down wood.
verse	12	6	5	But as one was felling a beam, the axe head fell into the water: and he cried, and said, Alas, master! for it was borrowed.
verse	12	6	6	@@And the man of God said, Where fell it? And he shewed him the place. And he cut down a stick, and cast @9it@7 in thither; and the iron did swim.
verse	12	6	7	@@Therefore said he, Take @9it@7 up to thee. And he put out his hand, and took it.
verse	12	6	8	@@Then the king of Syria warred against Israel, and took counsel with his servants, saying, In such and such a place @9shall be@7 my camp.
verse	12	6	9	And the man of God sent unto the king of Israel, saying, Beware that thou pass not such a place; for thither the Syrians are come down.
verse	12	6	10	And the king of Israel sent to the place which the man of God told him and warned him of, and saved himself there, not once nor twice.
verse	12	6	11	@@Therefore the heart of the king of Syria was sore troubled for this thing; and he called his servants, and said unto them, Will ye not shew me which of us @9is@7 for the king of Israel?
verse	12	6	12	@@And one of his servants said, None, my lord, O king: but Elisha, the prophet that @9is@7 in Israel, telleth the king of Israel the words that thou speakest in thy bedchamber.
verse	12	6	13	@@And he said, Go and spy where he @9is,@7 that I may send and fetch him. And it was told him, saying, Behold, @9he is@7 in Dothan.
verse	12	6	14	Therefore sent he thither horses, and chariots, and a great host: and they came by night, and compassed the city about.
verse	12	6	15	And when the servant of the man of God was risen early, and gone forth, behold, an host compassed the city both with horses and chariots. And his servant said unto him, Alas, my master! how shall we do?
verse	12	6	16	@@And he answered, Fear not: for they that @9be@7 with us @9are@7 more than they that @9be@7 with them.
verse	12	6	17	@@And Elisha prayed, and said, LORD, I pray thee, open his eyes, that he may see. And the LORD opened the eyes of the young man; and he saw: and, behold, the mountain @9was@7 full of horses and chariots of fire round about Elisha.
verse	12	6	18	And when they came down to him, Elisha prayed unto the LORD, and said, Smite this people, I pray thee, with blindness. And he smote them with blindness according to the word of Elisha.
verse	12	6	19	@@And Elisha said unto them, This @9is@7 not the way, neither @9is@7 this the city: follow me, and I will bring you to the man whom ye seek. But he led them to Samaria.
verse	12	6	20	@@And it came to pass, when they were come into Samaria, that Elisha said, LORD, open the eyes of these @9men,@7 that they may see. And the LORD opened their eyes, and they saw; and, behold, @9they were@7 in the midst of Samaria.
verse	12	6	21	@@And the king of Israel said unto Elisha, when he saw them, My father, shall I smite @9them?@7 shall I smite @9them? @7
verse	12	6	22	@@And he answered, Thou shalt not smite @9them:@7 wouldest thou smite those whom thou hast taken captive with thy sword and with thy bow? set bread and water before them, that they may eat and drink, and go to their master.
verse	12	6	23	And he prepared great provision for them: and when they had eaten and drunk, he sent them away, and they went to their master. So the bands of Syria came no more into the land of Israel.
verse	12	6	24	And it came to pass after this, that Benhadad king of Syria gathered all his host, and went up, and besieged Samaria.
verse	12	6	25	@@And there was a great famine in Samaria: and, behold, they besieged it, until an ass's head was @9sold@7 for fourscore @9pieces@7 of silver, and the fourth part of a cab of dove's dung for five @9pieces@7 of silver.
verse	12	6	26	And as the king of Israel was passing by upon the wall, there cried a woman unto him, saying, Help, my lord, O king.
verse	12	6	27	And he said, If the LORD do not help thee, whence shall I help thee? out of the barnfloor, or out of the winepress?
verse	12	6	28	And the king said unto her, What aileth thee? And she answered, This woman said unto me, Give thy son, that we may eat him to day, and we will eat my son to morrow.
verse	12	6	29	So we boiled my son, and did eat him: and I said unto her on the next day, Give thy son, that we may eat him: and she hath hid her son.
verse	12	6	30	@@And it came to pass, when the king heard the words of the woman, that he rent his clothes; and he passed by upon the wall, and the people looked, and, behold, @9he had@7 sackcloth within upon his flesh.
verse	12	6	31	Then he said, God do so and more also to me, if the head of Elisha the son of Shaphat shall stand on him this day.
verse	12	6	32	@@But Elisha sat in his house, and the elders sat with him; and @9the king@7 sent a man from before him: but ere the messenger came to him, he said to the elders, See ye how this son of a murderer hath sent to take away mine head? look, when the messenger cometh, shut the door, and hold him fast at the door: @9is@7 not the sound of his master's feet behind him?
verse	12	6	33	@@And while he yet talked with them, behold, the messenger came down unto him: and he said, Behold, this evil @9is@7 of the LORD; what should I wait for the LORD any longer?
verse	12	7	1	@@Then Elisha said, Hear ye the word of the LORD; Thus saith the LORD, To morrow about this time @9shall@7 a measure of fine flour @9be sold@7 for a shekel, and two measures of barley for a shekel, in the gate of Samaria.
verse	12	7	2	@@Then a lord on whose hand the king leaned answered the man of God, and said, Behold, @9if@7 the LORD would make windows in heaven, might this thing be? And he said, Behold, thou shalt see @9it@7 with thine eyes, but shalt not eat thereof.
verse	12	7	3	And there were four leprous men at the entering in of the gate: and they said one to another, Why sit we here until we die?
verse	12	7	4	@@If we say, We will enter into the city, then the famine @9is@7 in the city, and we shall die there: and if we sit still here, we die also. Now therefore come, and let us fall unto the host of the Syrians: if they save us alive, we shall live; and if they kill us, we shall but die.
verse	12	7	5	@@And they rose up in the twilight, to go unto the camp of the Syrians: and when they were come to the uttermost part of the camp of Syria, behold, @9there was@7 no man there.
verse	12	7	6	@@For the Lord had made the host of the Syrians to hear a noise of chariots, and a noise of horses, @9even@7 the noise of a great host: and they said one to another, Lo, the king of Israel hath hired against us the kings of the Hittites, and the kings of the Egyptians, to come upon us.
verse	12	7	7	@@Wherefore they arose and fled in the twilight, and left their tents, and their horses, and their asses, even the camp as it @9was,@7 and fled for their life.
verse	12	7	8	@@And when these lepers came to the uttermost part of the camp, they went into one tent, and did eat and drink, and carried thence silver, and gold, and raiment, and went and hid @9it;@7 and came again, and entered into another tent, and carried thence @9also,@7 and went and hid @9it. @7
verse	12	7	9	@@Then they said one to another, We do not well: this day @9is@7 a day of good tidings, and we hold our peace: if we tarry till the morning light, some mischief will come upon us: now therefore come, that we may go and tell the king's household.
verse	12	7	10	@@So they came and called unto the porter of the city: and they told them, saying, We came to the camp of the Syrians, and, behold, @9there was@7 no man there, neither voice of man, but horses tied, and asses tied, and the tents as they @9were. @7
verse	12	7	11	@@And he called the porters; and they told @9it@7 to the king's house within.
verse	12	7	12	@@And the king arose in the night, and said unto his servants, I will now shew you what the Syrians have done to us. They know that we @9be@7 hungry; therefore are they gone out of the camp to hide themselves in the field, saying, When they come out of the city, we shall catch them alive, and get into the city.
verse	12	7	13	@@And one of his servants answered and said, Let @9some@7 take, I pray thee, five of the horses that remain, which are left in the city, (behold, they @9are@7 as all the multitude of Israel that are left in it: behold, @9I say,@7 they @9are@7 even as all the multitude of the Israelites that are consumed:) and let us send and see.
verse	12	7	14	They took therefore two chariot horses; and the king sent after the host of the Syrians, saying, Go and see.
verse	12	7	15	@@And they went after them unto Jordan: and, lo, all the way @9was@7 full of garments and vessels, which the Syrians had cast away in their haste. And the messengers returned, and told the king.
