verse	18	6	27	@@Yea, ye overwhelm the fatherless, and ye dig @9a pit@7 for your friend.
verse	18	6	28	@@Now therefore be content, look upon me; for @9it is@7 evident unto you if I lie.
verse	18	6	29	@@Return, I pray you, let it not be iniquity; yea, return again, my righteousness @9is@7 in it.
verse	18	6	30	Is there iniquity in my tongue? cannot my taste discern perverse things?
verse	18	7	1	@@@9Is there@7 not an appointed time to man upon earth? @9are not@7 his days also like the days of an hireling?
verse	18	7	2	@@As a servant earnestly desireth the shadow, and as an hireling looketh for @9the reward of@7 his work:
verse	18	7	3	So am I made to possess months of vanity, and wearisome nights are appointed to me.
verse	18	7	4	When I lie down, I say, When shall I arise, and the night be gone? and I am full of tossings to and fro unto the dawning of the day.
verse	18	7	5	My flesh is clothed with worms and clods of dust; my skin is broken, and become loathsome.
verse	18	7	6	My days are swifter than a weaver's shuttle, and are spent without hope.
verse	18	7	7	O remember that my life is wind: mine eye shall no more see good.
verse	18	7	8	@@The eye of him that hath seen me shall see me no @9more:@7 thine eyes @9are@7 upon me, and I @9am@7 not.
