verse	18	7	12	@@@9Am@7 I a sea, or a whale, that thou settest a watch over me?
verse	18	7	13	When I say, My bed shall comfort me, my couch shall ease my complaint;
verse	18	7	14	Then thou scarest me with dreams, and terrifiest me through visions:
verse	18	7	15	@@So that my soul chooseth strangling, @9and@7 death rather than my life.
verse	18	7	16	@@I loathe @9it;@7 I would not live alway: let me alone; for my days @9are@7 vanity.
verse	18	7	17	@@What @9is@7 man, that thou shouldest magnify him? and that thou shouldest set thine heart upon him?
verse	18	7	18	@@And @9that@7 thou shouldest visit him every morning, @9and@7 try him every moment?
verse	18	7	19	How long wilt thou not depart from me, nor let me alone till I swallow down my spittle?
verse	18	7	20	I have sinned; what shall I do unto thee, O thou preserver of men? why hast thou set me as a mark against thee, so that I am a burden to myself?
verse	18	7	21	@@And why dost thou not pardon my transgression, and take away mine iniquity? for now shall I sleep in the dust; and thou shalt seek me in the morning, but I @9shall@7 not @9be.@7
verse	18	8	1	Then answered Bildad the Shuhite, and said,
verse	18	8	2	@@How long wilt thou speak these @9things?@7 and @9how long shall@7 the words of thy mouth be like a strong wind?
verse	18	8	3	Doth God pervert judgment? or doth the Almighty pervert justice?
verse	18	8	4	If thy children have sinned against him, and he have cast them away for their transgression;
verse	18	8	5	If thou wouldest seek unto God betimes, and make thy supplication to the Almighty;
verse	18	8	6	@@If thou @9wert@7 pure and upright; surely now he would awake for thee, and make the habitation of thy righteousness prosperous.
verse	18	8	7	Though thy beginning was small, yet thy latter end should greatly increase.
verse	18	8	8	For enquire, I pray thee, of the former age, and prepare thyself to the search of their fathers:
verse	18	8	9	@@(For we @9are but of@7 yesterday, and know nothing, because our days upon earth @9are@7 a shadow:)
verse	18	8	10	@@Shall not they teach thee, @9and@7 tell thee, and utter words out of their heart?
verse	18	8	11	Can the rush grow up without mire? can the flag grow without water?
verse	18	8	12	@@Whilst it @9is@7 yet in his greenness, @9and@7 not cut down, it withereth before any @9other@7 herb.
verse	18	8	13	@@So @9are@7 the paths of all that forget God; and the hypocrite's hope shall perish:
verse	18	8	14	@@Whose hope shall be cut off, and whose trust @9shall be@7 a spider's web.
verse	18	8	15	He shall lean upon his house, but it shall not stand: he shall hold it fast, but it shall not endure.
verse	18	8	16	@@He @9is@7 green before the sun, and his branch shooteth forth in his garden.
verse	18	8	17	@@His roots are wrapped about the heap, @9and@7 seeth the place of stones.
verse	18	8	18	@@If he destroy him from his place, then @9it@7 shall deny him, @9saying,@7 I have not seen thee.
verse	18	8	19	@@Behold, this @9is@7 the joy of his way, and out of the earth shall others grow.
verse	18	8	20	@@Behold, God will not cast away a perfect @9man,@7 neither will he help the evil doers:
verse	18	8	21	Till he fill thy mouth with laughing, and thy lips with rejoicing.
verse	18	8	22	They that hate thee shall be clothed with shame; and the dwelling place of the wicked shall come to nought.
verse	18	9	1	Then Job answered and said,
verse	18	9	2	@@I know @9it is@7 so of a truth: but how should man be just with God?
verse	18	9	3	If he will contend with him, he cannot answer him one of a thousand.
verse	18	9	4	@@@9He is@7 wise in heart, and mighty in strength: who hath hardened @9himself@7 against him, and hath prospered?
verse	18	9	5	Which removeth the mountains, and they know not: which overturneth them in his anger.
verse	18	9	6	Which shaketh the earth out of her place, and the pillars thereof tremble.
verse	18	9	7	Which commandeth the sun, and it riseth not; and sealeth up the stars.
verse	18	9	8	Which alone spreadeth out the heavens, and treadeth upon the waves of the sea.
verse	18	9	9	Which maketh Arcturus, Orion, and Pleiades, and the chambers of the south.
verse	18	9	10	Which doeth great things past finding out; yea, and wonders without number.
verse	18	9	11	@@Lo, he goeth by me, and I see @9him@7 not: he passeth on also, but I perceive him not.
verse	18	9	12	Behold, he taketh away, who can hinder him? who will say unto him, What doest thou?
verse	18	9	13	@@@9If@7 God will not withdraw his anger, the proud helpers do stoop under him.
verse	18	9	14	@@How much less shall I answer him, @9and@7 choose out my words @9to reason@7 with him?
verse	18	9	15	@@Whom, though I were righteous, @9yet@7 would I not answer, @9but@7 I would make supplication to my judge.
verse	18	9	16	@@If I had called, and he had answered me; @9yet@7 would I not believe that he had hearkened unto my voice.
verse	18	9	17	For he breaketh me with a tempest, and multiplieth my wounds without cause.
verse	18	9	18	He will not suffer me to take my breath, but filleth me with bitterness.
verse	18	9	19	@@If @9I speak@7 of strength, lo, @9he is@7 strong: and if of judgment, who shall set me a time @9to plead? @7
verse	18	9	20	@@If I justify myself, mine own mouth shall condemn me: @9if I say, I am@7 perfect, it shall also prove me perverse.
verse	18	9	21	@@@9Though@7 I @9were@7 perfect, @9yet@7 would I not know my soul: I would despise my life.
verse	18	9	22	@@This @9is@7 one @9thing,@7 therefore I said @9it,@7 He destroyeth the perfect and the wicked.
verse	18	9	23	If the scourge slay suddenly, he will laugh at the trial of the innocent.
verse	18	9	24	@@The earth is given into the hand of the wicked: he covereth the faces of the judges thereof; if not, where, @9and@7 who @9is@7 he?
verse	18	9	25	Now my days are swifter than a post: they flee away, they see no good.
verse	18	9	26	@@They are passed away as the swift ships: as the eagle @9that@7 hasteth to the prey.
verse	18	9	27	@@If I say, I will forget my complaint, I will leave off my heaviness, and comfort @9myself: @7
