verse	18	9	29	@@@9If@7 I be wicked, why then labour I in vain?
verse	18	9	30	If I wash myself with snow water, and make my hands never so clean;
verse	18	9	31	Yet shalt thou plunge me in the ditch, and mine own clothes shall abhor me.
verse	18	9	32	@@For @9he is@7 not a man, as I @9am, that@7 I should answer him, @9and@7 we should come together in judgment.
verse	18	9	33	@@Neither is there any daysman betwixt us, @9that@7 might lay his hand upon us both.
verse	18	9	34	Let him take his rod away from me, and let not his fear terrify me:
verse	18	9	35	@@@9Then@7 would I speak, and not fear him; but @9it is@7 not so with me.
verse	18	10	1	My soul is weary of my life; I will leave my complaint upon myself; I will speak in the bitterness of my soul.
verse	18	10	2	I will say unto God, Do not condemn me; shew me wherefore thou contendest with me.
verse	18	10	3	@@@9Is it@7 good unto thee that thou shouldest oppress, that thou shouldest despise the work of thine hands, and shine upon the counsel of the wicked?
verse	18	10	4	Hast thou eyes of flesh? or seest thou as man seeth?
verse	18	10	5	@@@9Are@7 thy days as the days of man? @9are@7 thy years as man's days,
verse	18	10	6	That thou enquirest after mine iniquity, and searchest after my sin?
verse	18	10	7	@@Thou knowest that I am not wicked; and @9there is@7 none that can deliver out of thine hand.
verse	18	10	8	Thine hands have made me and fashioned me together round about; yet thou dost destroy me.
verse	18	10	9	Remember, I beseech thee, that thou hast made me as the clay; and wilt thou bring me into dust again?
verse	18	10	10	Hast thou not poured me out as milk, and curdled me like cheese?
verse	18	10	11	Thou hast clothed me with skin and flesh, and hast fenced me with bones and sinews.
verse	18	10	12	Thou hast granted me life and favour, and thy visitation hath preserved my spirit.
verse	18	10	13	@@And these @9things@7 hast thou hid in thine heart: I know that this @9is@7 with thee.
verse	18	10	14	If I sin, then thou markest me, and thou wilt not acquit me from mine iniquity.
verse	18	10	15	@@If I be wicked, woe unto me; and @9if@7 I be righteous, @9yet@7 will I not lift up my head. @9I am@7 full of confusion; therefore see thou mine affliction;
verse	18	10	16	For it increaseth. Thou huntest me as a fierce lion: and again thou shewest thyself marvellous upon me.
verse	18	10	17	@@Thou renewest thy witnesses against me, and increasest thine indignation upon me; changes and war @9are@7 against me.
verse	18	10	18	Wherefore then hast thou brought me forth out of the womb? Oh that I had given up the ghost, and no eye had seen me!
verse	18	10	19	I should have been as though I had not been; I should have been carried from the womb to the grave.
verse	18	10	20	@@@9Are@7 not my days few? cease @9then, and@7 let me alone, that I may take comfort a little,
verse	18	10	21	@@Before I go @9whence@7 I shall not return, @9even@7 to the land of darkness and the shadow of death;
verse	18	10	22	@@A land of darkness, as darkness @9itself; and@7 of the shadow of death, without any order, and @9where@7 the light @9is@7 as darkness.
verse	18	11	1	Then answered Zophar the Naamathite, and said,
verse	18	11	2	Should not the multitude of words be answered? and should a man full of talk be justified?
verse	18	11	3	Should thy lies make men hold their peace? and when thou mockest, shall no man make thee ashamed?
verse	18	11	4	@@For thou hast said, My doctrine @9is@7 pure, and I am clean in thine eyes.
verse	18	11	5	But oh that God would speak, and open his lips against thee;
verse	18	11	6	@@And that he would shew thee the secrets of wisdom, that @9they are@7 double to that which is! Know therefore that God exacteth of thee @9less@7 than thine iniquity @9deserveth. @7
verse	18	11	7	Canst thou by searching find out God? canst thou find out the Almighty unto perfection?
verse	18	11	8	@@@9It is@7 as high as heaven; what canst thou do? deeper than hell; what canst thou know?
verse	18	11	9	@@The measure thereof @9is@7 longer than the earth, and broader than the sea.
verse	18	11	10	If he cut off, and shut up, or gather together, then who can hinder him?
verse	18	11	11	@@For he knoweth vain men: he seeth wickedness also; will he not then consider @9it? @7
verse	18	11	12	@@For vain man would be wise, though man be born @9like@7 a wild ass's colt.
verse	18	11	13	If thou prepare thine heart, and stretch out thine hands toward him;
verse	18	11	14	@@If iniquity @9be@7 in thine hand, put it far away, and let not wickedness dwell in thy tabernacles.
verse	18	11	15	For then shalt thou lift up thy face without spot; yea, thou shalt be stedfast, and shalt not fear:
verse	18	11	16	@@Because thou shalt forget @9thy@7 misery, @9and@7 remember @9it@7 as waters @9that@7 pass away:
verse	18	11	17	@@And @9thine@7 age shall be clearer than the noonday; thou shalt shine forth, thou shalt be as the morning.
verse	18	11	18	@@And thou shalt be secure, because there is hope; yea, thou shalt dig @9about thee, and@7 thou shalt take thy rest in safety.
verse	18	11	19	@@Also thou shalt lie down, and none shall make @9thee@7 afraid; yea, many shall make suit unto thee.
verse	18	11	20	@@But the eyes of the wicked shall fail, and they shall not escape, and their hope @9shall be as@7 the giving up of the ghost.
verse	18	12	1	And Job answered and said,
verse	18	12	2	@@No doubt but ye @9are@7 the people, and wisdom shall die with you.
verse	18	12	3	@@But I have understanding as well as you; I @9am@7 not inferior to you: yea, who knoweth not such things as these?
verse	18	12	4	@@I am @9as@7 one mocked of his neighbour, who calleth upon God, and he answereth him: the just upright @9man is@7 laughed to scorn.
verse	18	12	5	@@He that is ready to slip with @9his@7 feet @9is as@7 a lamp despised in the thought of him that is at ease.
verse	18	12	6	@@The tabernacles of robbers prosper, and they that provoke God are secure; into whose hand God bringeth @9abundantly. @7
verse	18	12	7	But ask now the beasts, and they shall teach thee; and the fowls of the air, and they shall tell thee:
verse	18	12	8	Or speak to the earth, and it shall teach thee: and the fishes of the sea shall declare unto thee.
verse	18	12	9	Who knoweth not in all these that the hand of the LORD hath wrought this?
verse	18	12	10	@@In whose hand @9is@7 the soul of every living thing, and the breath of all mankind.
verse	18	12	11	Doth not the ear try words? and the mouth taste his meat?
verse	18	12	12	@@With the ancient @9is@7 wisdom; and in length of days understanding.
verse	18	12	13	@@With him @9is@7 wisdom and strength, he hath counsel and understanding.
verse	18	12	14	Behold, he breaketh down, and it cannot be built again: he shutteth up a man, and there can be no opening.
verse	18	12	15	Behold, he withholdeth the waters, and they dry up: also he sendeth them out, and they overturn the earth.
verse	18	12	16	@@With him @9is@7 strength and wisdom: the deceived and the deceiver @9are@7 his.
verse	18	12	17	He leadeth counsellors away spoiled, and maketh the judges fools.
verse	18	12	18	He looseth the bond of kings, and girdeth their loins with a girdle.
verse	18	12	19	He leadeth princes away spoiled, and overthroweth the mighty.
verse	18	12	20	He removeth away the speech of the trusty, and taketh away the understanding of the aged.
verse	18	12	21	He poureth contempt upon princes, and weakeneth the strength of the mighty.
verse	18	12	22	He discovereth deep things out of darkness, and bringeth out to light the shadow of death.
verse	18	12	23	@@He increaseth the nations, and destroyeth them: he enlargeth the nations, and straiteneth them @9again. @7
verse	18	12	24	@@He taketh away the heart of the chief of the people of the earth, and causeth them to wander in a wilderness @9where there is@7 no way.
verse	18	12	25	@@They grope in the dark without light, and he maketh them to stagger like @9a@7 drunken @9man.@7
verse	18	13	1	@@Lo, mine eye hath seen all @9this,@7 mine ear hath heard and understood it.
verse	18	13	2	@@What ye know, @9the same@7 do I know also: I @9am@7 not inferior unto you.
verse	18	13	3	Surely I would speak to the Almighty, and I desire to reason with God.
verse	18	13	4	@@But ye @9are@7 forgers of lies, ye @9are@7 all physicians of no value.
verse	18	13	5	Oh that ye would altogether hold your peace! and it should be your wisdom.
verse	18	13	6	Hear now my reasoning, and hearken to the pleadings of my lips.
verse	18	13	7	Will ye speak wickedly for God? and talk deceitfully for him?
verse	18	13	8	Will ye accept his person? will ye contend for God?
verse	18	13	9	@@Is it good that he should search you out? or as one man mocketh another, do ye @9so@7 mock him?
verse	18	13	10	He will surely reprove you, if ye do secretly accept persons.
verse	18	13	11	Shall not his excellency make you afraid? and his dread fall upon you?
verse	18	13	12	@@Your remembrances @9are@7 like unto ashes, your bodies to bodies of clay.
verse	18	13	13	@@Hold your peace, let me alone, that I may speak, and let come on me what @9will. @7
verse	18	13	14	Wherefore do I take my flesh in my teeth, and put my life in mine hand?
verse	18	13	15	Though he slay me, yet will I trust in him: but I will maintain mine own ways before him.
verse	18	13	16	@@He also @9shall be@7 my salvation: for an hypocrite shall not come before him.
verse	18	13	17	Hear diligently my speech, and my declaration with your ears.
verse	18	13	18	@@Behold now, I have ordered @9my@7 cause; I know that I shall be justified.
verse	18	13	19	@@Who @9is@7 he @9that@7 will plead with me? for now, if I hold my tongue, I shall give up the ghost.
verse	18	13	20	@@Only do not two @9things@7 unto me: then will I not hide myself from thee.
verse	18	13	21	Withdraw thine hand far from me: and let not thy dread make me afraid.
verse	18	13	22	Then call thou, and I will answer: or let me speak, and answer thou me.
verse	18	13	23	@@How many @9are@7 mine iniquities and sins? make me to know my transgression and my sin.
verse	18	13	24	Wherefore hidest thou thy face, and holdest me for thine enemy?
verse	18	13	25	Wilt thou break a leaf driven to and fro? and wilt thou pursue the dry stubble?
verse	18	13	26	For thou writest bitter things against me, and makest me to possess the iniquities of my youth.
verse	18	13	27	Thou puttest my feet also in the stocks, and lookest narrowly unto all my paths; thou settest a print upon the heels of my feet.
verse	18	13	28	And he, as a rotten thing, consumeth, as a garment that is moth eaten.
verse	18	14	1	@@Man @9that is@7 born of a woman @9is@7 of few days, and full of trouble.
verse	18	14	2	He cometh forth like a flower, and is cut down: he fleeth also as a shadow, and continueth not.
verse	18	14	3	And dost thou open thine eyes upon such an one, and bringest me into judgment with thee?
verse	18	14	4	@@Who can bring a clean @9thing@7 out of an unclean? not one.
verse	18	14	5	@@Seeing his days @9are@7 determined, the number of his months @9are@7 with thee, thou hast appointed his bounds that he cannot pass;
verse	18	14	6	Turn from him, that he may rest, till he shall accomplish, as an hireling, his day.
verse	18	14	7	For there is hope of a tree, if it be cut down, that it will sprout again, and that the tender branch thereof will not cease.
verse	18	14	8	Though the root thereof wax old in the earth, and the stock thereof die in the ground;
verse	18	14	9	@@@9Yet@7 through the scent of water it will bud, and bring forth boughs like a plant.
verse	18	14	10	@@But man dieth, and wasteth away: yea, man giveth up the ghost, and where @9is@7 he?
verse	18	14	11	@@@9As@7 the waters fail from the sea, and the flood decayeth and drieth up:
verse	18	14	12	@@So man lieth down, and riseth not: till the heavens @9be@7 no more, they shall not awake, nor be raised out of their sleep.
verse	18	14	13	Oh that thou wouldest hide me in the grave, that thou wouldest keep me secret, until thy wrath be past, that thou wouldest appoint me a set time, and remember me!
verse	18	14	14	@@If a man die, shall he live @9again?@7 all the days of my appointed time will I wait, till my change come.
verse	18	14	15	Thou shalt call, and I will answer thee: thou wilt have a desire to the work of thine hands.
verse	18	14	16	For now thou numberest my steps: dost thou not watch over my sin?
verse	18	14	17	@@My transgression @9is@7 sealed up in a bag, and thou sewest up mine iniquity.
verse	18	14	18	And surely the mountain falling cometh to nought, and the rock is removed out of his place.
verse	18	14	19	@@The waters wear the stones: thou washest away the things which grow @9out@7 of the dust of the earth; and thou destroyest the hope of man.
